{
  "dom_id": "step-e9927",
  "name": "Example: Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated",
  "problem": "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 + 14x + 49) - 8$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+14x+49 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+14x+{7}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 7x+{7}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+14x+49)-8 & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2}-8 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
